1. LangGraph: yes, with a specific justification

Use LangGraph, not plain LangChain chains. The reason has to be one you can defend. "It adds abstraction" won't survive an interviewer asking "why not 50 lines of Python?" But LangGraph has three features that fit this exact problem:

-   Human-in-the-loop is built in. LangGraph's interrupt() can pause the graph at the "Needs CA review" step, wait for the CA's decision, and resume. That's your human checkpoint as an actual part of the system, not a note on a diagram.
-   Checkpointing gives you the audit trail. Every state transition (inputs, check results, LLM outputs, CA override) is saved, so "trace every output back to its source" comes almost for free.
-   The graph is the architecture diagram. Nodes and conditional edges map one-to-one to what you'll draw in the brief: section16_checks → classify_17_5 → validate_citations → route → (auto | CA review) → report.

Guardrails to keep it defensible:

-   Keep the Section 16 checks as plain Python functions inside graph nodes. LangGraph only orchestrates; it never decides a statutory condition.
